        still passes with the new filter bar's flex-wrap in every page

## 4. Proven, not just passing once

17 tests for this slice: `filterAndSortIncidents` against a fixed
3-incident fixture (each severity/family/sort/search case asserting the
*exact* resulting id order, not just a count), five component tests
(search narrows the rendered list, the distinct no-matches empty state,
a checkbox click calling `navigate` with the exact patched context, and
- specifically - unchecking an already-selected severity removing just
that one value rather than clearing the whole filter array).

The severity-sort rank table was deliberately inverted
(`{error:3,warn:2,notice:1,info:0}`) to confirm the sort test actually
depends on the real ranking rather than passing by coincidence of the
fixture's insertion order - failed with the exact wrong order, then
passed again once reverted.

## 5. Actually looked at it

Fresh browser tab, demo recording, both languages:
- Checking "error" alone: list narrows to error-severity incidents only,
  URL becomes `#/incidents?severities=error`.
- Adding a "gateway" search on top: narrows further to exactly "The
  default gateway is being answered by a different machine", URL becomes
  `#/incidents?severities=error&query=gateway` - both filters compose
  (AND), matching the unit test's expectation exactly.
- Switching sort to "Most severe first": URL becomes
  `#/incidents?sort=severity`, and the first incident shown is the
  highest-severity one (a DHCP rogue-server incident, error, 92%
  confidence) - the list genuinely re-sorted, not just the dropdown's own
  display value changing.
- Arabic mode: all labels correctly translated ("الخطورة:", "الفئة:",
  "الترتيب", family names via the existing kind-catalogue's
  `labelForFamily`), and the sort selection from English mode survived
  the language switch (route context and UI language are correctly
  independent state).
- Console clean throughout.

## 6. What "master/detail" would still need, and why it is not in this slice

The execution order's Phase 5 line names both "master/detail" and
"search/sort/filter" for Incidents in one breath. This slice delivers
search/sort/filter completely and lets every filtered incident keep
rendering as a fully-expanded `IncidentCard` (the layout already in
place, which the previous two slices' new features - evidence
disclosure, export - were built directly on top of). A true master/
detail split (a compact list, click to see one incident's full detail in
a separate panel) is a distinct layout paradigm change affecting how
every existing feature on this page presents, not an additive feature -
attempting it inside this same slice risked conflating two different
kinds of change and made the diff harder to verify on its own terms.
Tracked as the remaining half of this Phase 5 line, not silently folded
into "done" by this commit.

## 7. What remains in Phase 5

Incidents master/detail layout (§6); Rules bilingual catalogue
improvements; Settings regrouped (the three groups that can be built
now, per the previous slice's note) with a sticky save/discard bar and
unsaved-changes guard. No new ADR - client-side filtering/sorting over
data already in memory, using routing fields that already existed.
